  "romFile": "game",
  "deterministic": true,
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    256,
    16,
    64,
    256,
    2560
  ],
  "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. The GameCube itself is small (24MB main RAM + 16MB ARAM + caches, all anonymous mmaps since the sandbox has no shared memory), but dolphin's own working set - the software rasterizer's EFB, texture and vertex machinery, DiscIO buffers - lives in the mmap arena too, which musl routes every large allocation into.",
  "video": {
    "gpuStatesSurviveTheContext": true,
    "_comment_gpuStates": "TRUE, and measured so. A state this core makes opens again in a later session: a run reopened onto one is byte-identical to a run that never stopped - System RAM, ARAM, the L1 cache, the emitted audio and every pixel - at 40, 120 and 240 frames past the load. Two things had to be true for that. The renderer rebuilds its GL objects when the calls land on a context that is not the one they came from (the bridge's GL_OP_CONTEXT_ID), and it leaves a framebuffer bound afterwards, without which the machine's first draw dereferences a null one. And the picture is presented by decoding the XFB from the machine's own memory rather than from a copy of it kept in VRAM: that copy is the crisp one, rendered at internal resolution, and it exists nowhere in a savestate, so a reopened run had only the RAM decode and drew a different picture from there on - 62% of the pixels 40 frames later, 99.7% after 240, never recovering. Presenting from RAM costs the upscaled picture and agrees with the software renderer, which is the machine's own answer. Above 1x Internal Resolution (chimera#149, by the user's decision) the picture IS the crisp copy in VRAM again, because that is the point of the setting: the machine is unchanged by where the picture comes from (measured: the RAM at field 3000 is the same either way), and a movie played from power-on is crisp throughout; right after a state load the picture may differ from a run that never stopped. At 1x, and on the software renderer, the picture still comes from the machine's memory.",
    "_comment": "buffer CAPACITY: 4x the machine's largest field (720x576), for Internal Resolution 4x on opengl-hw; the live size comes from GetVideoWidth/Height per frame, which follow the VI field being scanned out (640x480 NTSC progressive, 640x287-288 as one PAL/NTSC interlaced field). The static vsync fraction is the NTSC default; the runtime GetVsyncNumerator/Denominator exports follow the machine's actual video mode, PAL included.",
    "width": 2880,
    "height": 2304,
    "virtualWidth": 640,
    "virtualHeight": 480,
    "vsyncNumerator": 60000,
    "vsyncDenominator": 1001,
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra"
  },
  "audio": {
    "_comment": "the mixer's 48kHz stereo output, drained by machine time: rate * den / num samples per field with the remainder accumulated, so a PAL field is exactly 960 pairs and nothing is ever lost or invented.",
    "rate": 48000,
    "samplesPerFrame": 16384,
    "channels": 2,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "lag": {
    "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
  },
  "settings": [
    {
      "name": "machine",
      "display": "Machine",
      "description": "Which console this project is. One core.wbx is both; this is the choice, pinned in the project and recorded in the movie - the emulator never guesses it from the image, and an image of the other console is a load error, not a silent boot of the wrong machine. A GameCube has memory card slots; a Wii keeps its saves in NAND. 'triforce' is the arcade board Sega, Namco and Nintendo built on a GameCube (Virtua Striker, F-Zero AX, Mario Kart Arcade GP): its disc image carries the mark, and a project must say triforce to boot one.",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "gamecube",
        "wii",
        "triforce"
      ],
      "default": "gamecube"
    },
    {
      "name": "port1",
      "display": "Controller in Port 1",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "gc-controller"
      ],
      "default": "gc-controller",
      "description": "What is plugged into controller port 1. An empty port answers the SerialInterface the way a real empty port does - games notice - and its buttons and sticks leave the input roll entirely, because a control the machine does not have is not a column and not a character in a movie.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "gamecube",
          "wii"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "port2",
      "display": "Controller in Port 2",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "gc-controller"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into controller port 2. An empty port answers the SerialInterface the way a real empty port does - games notice - and its buttons and sticks leave the input roll entirely, because a control the machine does not have is not a column and not a character in a movie.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "gamecube",
          "wii"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "port3",
      "display": "Controller in Port 3",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "gc-controller"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into controller port 3. An empty port answers the SerialInterface the way a real empty port does - games notice - and its buttons and sticks leave the input roll entirely, because a control the machine does not have is not a column and not a character in a movie.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "gamecube",
          "wii"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "port4",
      "display": "Controller in Port 4",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "gc-controller"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into controller port 4. An empty port answers the SerialInterface the way a real empty port does - games notice - and its buttons and sticks leave the input roll entirely, because a control the machine does not have is not a column and not a character in a movie.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "gamecube",
          "wii"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "memcard_a",
      "display": "Memory Card in Slot A",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": true,
      "description": "Whether the machine has a card in its first slot. Games ask on their first screen and behave differently without one, so this is part of the machine. The card itself is not a file here: it arrives from, and returns to, the project's save data.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "gamecube"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "widescreen",
      "display": "Widescreen (Wii)",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": false,
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "wii"
      },
      "description": "The Wii's own system setting 'Screen: Widescreen' (the SYSCONF's IPL.AR). A Wii game reads it to decide whether to draw for a 16:9 screen or a 4:3 one, and many draw differently for each - a wider view, or the same view letterboxed - so it is part of the machine and a movie records it. Off is 4:3, which is what a console fresh out of the box says. The picture Chimera shows stays the machine's own 640 pixels wide either way; set Config > Display > Aspect Ratio Selection > 'Use custom AR' to 16:9 to see a widescreen game the shape it was drawn for."
    },
    {
      "name": "cpu_core",
      "display": "CPU Emulation",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "interpreter",
        "cached-interpreter",
        "jit"
      ],
      "default": "jit",
      "description": "How the PowerPC runs. All three are deterministic and each is byte-identical between the native and sandboxed builds - but they are NOT identical to each other: the cached interpreter and the JIT account time in blocks where the interpreter counts every instruction, so the machine's timing differs and a movie made under one does not replay under another. That is why this is a machine setting the movie cites rather than a convenience. 'jit' compiles PowerPC to native code (without fastmem or fault tricks, which the sandbox cannot host and determinism would not want) and is the default because it is about five times faster; 'cached-interpreter' groups instructions into predecoded blocks; 'interpreter' is the slowest and the longest-proven."
    },
    {
      "name": "renderer",
      "display": "Renderer",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "software",
        "opengl-hw"
      ],
      "default": "opengl-hw",
      "description": "Which renderer draws the picture. 'software' is dolphin's software rasterizer: fully inside the sandbox, byte-identical everywhere, the renderer every determinism claim is built on - pick it when a movie must replay on machines unlike yours. 'opengl-hw' is dolphin's own OpenGL backend driving a REAL GPU through the bridge - much faster and the way to see games at their best, but the GPU is outside the sandbox and different on every machine: EFB and XFB copies land back in RAM (so the machine's memory is still the machine's), and a movie recorded this way replays only on the same driver. Without a GPU the machine falls back to software and says so."
    },
    {
      "name": "mmu",
      "display": "Enable MMU",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": false,
      "description": "Dolphin's full MMU emulation (Config > Advanced > Enable MMU). A few games need it to run correctly, and TASVideos asks for it on those (see chimera#149). Off is dolphin's default and costs nothing; on is slower. It is the CPU's own behaviour, on either renderer, so a movie cites it and needs the same value to play back."
    },
    {
      "name": "internal_resolution",
      "display": "Internal Resolution",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "1x",
        "2x",
        "3x",
        "4x"
      ],
      "default": "1x",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "renderer",
        "is": "opengl-hw"
      },
      "description": "How many times the console's resolution the GPU renders at (Graphics > Enhancements > Internal Resolution). Above 1x the picture Chimera shows and encodes is the crisp one the GPU drew - 2x of a 640x448 game is 1280x896 - instead of the console's own 640-wide framebuffer. It changes what the machine computes, not just what you see: the game reads back its own picture (EFB and XFB copies land in the console's memory), so a movie made with one value may not play back with another. Choose it when you create the project; changing it later is a structural edit. Measured on Virtua Striker 2002: 2x against 1x changes RAM from the first 3D frame and the lag count by field 3000 (547 against 585). One cost of the crisp picture: it is not part of a savestate, so right after a state load the picture can look different from the run that never stopped; the machine does not, and a movie played from power-on - an encode - is crisp throughout."
    },
    {
      "name": "msaa",
      "display": "Anti-Aliasing (MSAA)",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "off",
        "2x",
        "4x",
        "8x"
      ],
      "default": "off",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "renderer",
        "is": "opengl-hw"
      },
      "description": "Multisample anti-aliasing (Graphics > Enhancements > Anti-Aliasing). Smooths the edges of what the GPU draws. It changes what the machine computes, not just what you see: the game reads back its own picture (EFB and XFB copies land in the console's memory), so a movie made with one value may not play back with another. Choose it when you create the project; changing it later is a structural edit."
    },
    {
      "name": "ssaa",
      "display": "Supersampling (SSAA)",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": false,
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "renderer",
        "is": "opengl-hw"
      },
      "description": "Turns the multisampling above into supersampling (dolphin's SSAA variants of Anti-Aliasing): every sample is shaded, which also smooths texture and shader edges. Does nothing while Anti-Aliasing is off. It changes what the machine computes, not just what you see: the game reads back its own picture (EFB and XFB copies land in the console's memory), so a movie made with one value may not play back with another. Choose it when you create the project; changing it later is a structural edit."
    },
    {
      "name": "anisotropy",
      "display": "Anisotropic Filtering",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "default",
        "1x",
        "2x",
        "4x",
        "8x",
        "16x"
      ],
      "default": "default",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "renderer",
        "is": "opengl-hw"
      },
      "description": "Anisotropic texture filtering (Graphics > Enhancements). 'default' leaves each texture as the game set it up. It changes what the machine computes, not just what you see: the game reads back its own picture (EFB and XFB copies land in the console's memory), so a movie made with one value may not play back with another. Choose it when you create the project; changing it later is a structural edit."
    },
    {
      "name": "texture_filtering",
      "display": "Texture Filtering",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "default",
        "nearest",
        "linear"
      ],
      "default": "default",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "renderer",
        "is": "opengl-hw"
      },
      "description": "Forces every texture to nearest or linear filtering (Graphics > Enhancements > Texture Filtering). 'default' leaves each texture as the game set it up. It changes what the machine computes, not just what you see: the game reads back its own picture (EFB and XFB copies land in the console's memory), so a movie made with one value may not play back with another. Choose it when you create the project; changing it later is a structural edit."
    },
    {
      "name": "widescreen_hack",
      "display": "Widescreen Hack",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": false,
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "renderer",
        "is": "opengl-hw"
      },
      "description": "Dolphin's widescreen hack (Graphics > General): the game's 3D view is widened to fill a 16:9 screen. The picture stays the machine's own width, squeezed - set Config > Display > Aspect Ratio to 16:9 to see it as meant. The 2D parts of a game (menus, HUD) are not widened, and may stretch. It changes what the machine computes, not just what you see: the game reads back its own picture (EFB and XFB copies land in the console's memory), so a movie made with one value may not play back with another. Choose it when you create the project; changing it later is a structural edit."
    },
    {
      "name": "texture_cache",
      "display": "Texture Cache Accuracy",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "safe",
        "medium",
        "fast"
      ],
      "default": "fast",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "renderer",
        "is": "opengl-hw"
      },
      "description": "How closely dolphin checks whether a texture in the game's memory has changed (Graphics > Hacks > Texture Cache Accuracy). 'safe' checks all of it and catches games that change a texture in place; 'fast' is dolphin's default. No difference was seen on the games tried here (up to 3000 fields, 3D included), but where a game does change textures in place, 'safe' draws it right and the others may not. It changes what the machine computes, not just what you see: the game reads back its own picture (EFB and XFB copies land in the console's memory), so a movie made with one value may not play back with another. Choose it when you create the project; changing it later is a structural edit."
    },
